# Squash Booking Project — Code Explanation

A file-by-file breakdown of what the code does and why it's built that way. Use this as reference material alongside the presentation script — it goes into more technical depth than the speaking script does.

---

## Backend

### `server.js` — API entry point

This is where the Express app is assembled and started.

```js
require('dotenv').config();
```
Loads environment variables (DB credentials, JWT secret, port) from a `.env` file. Keeps secrets out of the codebase entirely — nothing sensitive is ever hardcoded or committed to git.

```js
app.use(cors());
```
Enables Cross-Origin Resource Sharing. During development, the React frontend (Vite dev server) and the Express backend run on different ports, which browsers treat as different "origins." Without CORS enabled, the browser would block the frontend's requests to the API.

```js
app.use(express.json());
```
Built-in Express middleware that parses incoming JSON request bodies into `req.body`. Without this, `req.body` would be `undefined` on POST requests.

```js
app.use('/api/courts', courtsRouter);
app.use('/api/bookings', bookingsRouter);
app.use('/api/booking', bookingsRouter);
```
Each resource (courts, bookings) gets its own router file rather than every endpoint living in one giant file. This is standard REST API structure — it scales cleanly as more resources get added. The extra `/api/booking` (singular) line is a backwards-compatibility shim: it points to the exact same router as `/api/bookings`, so if any older client code called the singular route, it still works without duplicating logic.

```js
app.get('/test-db', async (req, res) => { ... });
```
A diagnostic route that queries the `courts` table directly. Useful for confirming the database connection works independent of any frontend — you can hit this in a browser or Postman and immediately know if the DB layer is healthy.

**Why this matters:** separating concerns (parsing, CORS, routing) into distinct middleware and router files means each piece can be tested, changed, or debugged independently.

