### `Connection.js` — database connection

```js
const mysql = require('mysql2/promise');
```
Uses the promise-based version of the `mysql2` driver, so database queries can be written with `async`/`await` instead of nested callbacks.

```js
const pool = mysql.createPool({
  host: ...,
  user: ...,
  password: ...,
  database: ...,
  waitForConnections: true,
  connectionLimit: 10,
  queueLimit: 0
});
```
This creates a **connection pool**, not a single connection. The difference matters a lot in practice:

- A single connection means every request has to wait its turn to use that one connection to the database.
- A pool keeps multiple connections (up to 10 here) open and ready, so several requests can query the database at the same time.

`waitForConnections: true` means that if all 10 connections are busy, a new request queues and waits rather than failing immediately. `queueLimit: 0` means that queue has no cap — it'll wait indefinitely rather than reject with an error under heavy load (a tradeoff worth knowing: it favors availability over fast-failing).

**Why this matters:** booking systems are exactly the kind of app where concurrent requests are common — multiple users checking availability or submitting bookings around the same moment. A pool handles that far better than a single shared connection.

