## Frontend

### `vite.config.ts` — dev server proxy

```js
server: {
  proxy: {
    '/api': {
      target: 'http://localhost:5000',
      changeOrigin: true,
    },
  },
}
```
During development, the React app (Vite's dev server) and the Express API run on two different ports. This proxy rule means any frontend request to a path starting with `/api` gets silently forwarded to `localhost:5000`. The practical benefit: frontend code can just call `fetch('/api/bookings')` without hardcoding a port number, and it sidesteps CORS friction in local development entirely, since from the browser's perspective the request never left the Vite dev server's origin.

### `index.html` + `main.tsx` — entry point

Standard Vite/React scaffolding: a single `<div id="root">` in the HTML, and `main.tsx` mounts the React app into it as an ES module (`<script type="module" src="/src/main.tsx">`).

### `BookingPayload` type (TypeScript)

```ts
export interface BookingPayload {
  court_id: number;
  booking_date: string;
  start_time: string;
  num_players: 1 | 2;
  is_member: boolean;
  guardian_required: "Y" | "N";
  member_id: string | null;
  guest_name: string | null;
  guest_contact: string | null;
}
```
`num_players: 1 | 2` is a TypeScript **literal union type** — the compiler enforces that this field can only ever be `1` or `2`, not any other number. That's a compile-time guarantee, not just a runtime check, meaning a bug that tries to assign `num_players = 3` would be caught before the code even runs, not discovered later in production.

### Login-aware conditional rendering

```tsx
{!isLoggedIn && (
  <div className="toggle-row">
    <button onClick={() => setIsMember(true)}>Member</button>
    <button onClick={() => setIsMember(false)}>Guest</button>
  </div>
)}
```
The member/guest toggle only renders when `isLoggedIn` is false. A logged-in user's membership status isn't a choice — it's a fact derived from their session — so the UI doesn't ask a question that's already answered.

### Real-time slot status

```ts
const res = await fetch(`/api/bookings?court_id=${court}&date=${date}`);
const rows = await res.json();
```
Rather than a hardcoded list of booked slots, the schedule grid fetches live data from the backend whenever the selected court changes, and rebuilds the booked/available map from actual database rows. After a successful booking, the newly booked slot is also added to local state immediately — so the UI updates instantly without waiting for a full refetch, while the actual conflict protection still lives server-side.

